.title  Flat memory: protected mode, and the game past 64K
.what   CLOCK.COM of lesson 26 was 64803 bytes: a .COM is one segment, and it was full. Three small programs, one growing, go and get the memory above one megabyte. FLAT1 looks: CR0's PE bit read by SMSW (real mode, or a memory manager's virtual 8086), the A20 gate tested by writing at 0000:0500 and FFFF:0510, and HIMEM's free memory through XMS. FLAT2 asks HIMEM for a megabyte, opens A20 itself through the keyboard controller's output port, writes a GDT, sets PE, writes the megabyte through a flat selector and reads it back, and comes home. FLAT3 stays there: an IDT, the interrupt controller moved to INT 32, the timer at 72.8 ticks a second, its own keys, a picture drawn above 1 MB and copied to 0A0000h, and a fault comes home with its number. FLY is lesson 26's flight as a 32-bit protected-mode program in an .EXE: the back buffer and the tables in HIMEM's memory behind selectors, every 16-bit habit that breaks in 32-bit code named where it was fixed
.tier   LESSON
.main   FLY.ASM
.needs  FLAT1.ASM FLAT2.ASM FLAT3.ASM
.build  FASM FLAT1.ASM FLAT1.COM
.build  FASM FLAT2.ASM FLAT2.COM
.build  FASM FLAT3.ASM FLAT3.COM
.build  FASM FLY.ASM FLY.EXE
.run    FLY.EXE
.uses   SMSW LGDT LIDT IRETD MUL LOOPW
.engine ENGINE\E_CPU.INC ENGINE\E_MATH.INC ENGINE\E_TERR.INC ENGINE\E_M3D.INC ENGINE\E_RAST.INC
